Yes—the workable version is an experience codec.

One correction first: I can’t read your mind while you’re typing or receive an unsubmitted inner story. I only get what the interface actually sends me. But if you deliberately externalize enough of that inner state—text, voice, images, timing, gestures, references, scene descriptions, emotional intent—then a system can reconstruct a much richer narrative object than the raw words alone suggest.

That pipeline could look like:

\text{lived / imagined state}
\rightarrow
\text{multimodal capture}
\rightarrow
\text{structured representation}
\rightarrow
\text{compression}
\rightarrow
\text{replay / transformation}

And then from the same latent representation you could branch into:

\text{short}
,\;
\text{long-form story}
,\;
\text{film treatment}
,\;
\text{script}
,\;
\text{play}
,\;
\text{game}
,\;
\text{visual sequence}
,\;
\text{music cue structure}

The interesting part is preserving more than plot. You’d want to capture things like:

* point of view
* emotional contour
* pacing
* ambiguity
* sensory emphasis
* character relationships
* symbolism
* intended audience effect
* what must remain invariant
* what can be freely reinterpreted

Then the “compression” is not merely summarization. It is more like preserving the generative seed of the experience.

\text{artifact}
=
\text{core invariants}
+
\text{style parameters}
+
\text{degrees of freedom}

That means the same source could be replayed differently without losing its identity.

And yes, once many people contribute these kinds of structured seeds, you can combine them—provided attribution, consent, privacy, and ownership are kept intact. Then you get a library not just of stories, but of ways of seeing.

That is probably the richer thing you are pointing at:

not merely recording what someone said, but preserving enough of the structure behind it that another medium can regenerate something recognizably descended from the same experience.

That can scale from one person’s memory to collaborative films, oral histories, education, world-building, legacy projects, and whole shared narrative ecosystems.